# 🛡️ Security Lessons

| ❌ Vulnerability                       | ✅ Secure Practice                                     |
| ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Unrestricted file upload              | Allow only required file types                        |
| Server-side executable files accepted | Store uploads outside the executable web root         |
| Insufficient MIME/type validation     | Validate file extension, MIME type, and file contents |
| Uploaded files directly accessible    | Generate safe filenames and restrict execution        |
| Excessive `sudo` permissions          | Follow least-privilege principles                     |
| `www-data` has unrestricted sudo      | Never grant unnecessary administrative privileges     |

---

## 🔐 Key Takeaways

### 1. File Upload Validation Matters

A profile-picture feature should **never blindly trust the uploaded filename or extension**.

A malicious upload can turn a simple file-upload vulnerability into **Remote Code Execution**.

### 2. Always Check Privileges After Obtaining a Shell

```bash
sudo -l
```

can reveal whether the current user has elevated privileges.

### 3. Least Privilege Is Critical

Giving a web-server account:

```text
NOPASSWD: ALL
```

effectively removes a major security boundary.

